**Git (you own it):**
You edit, test, stage, and commit. The host owns only the pull request.

- Work only in `{{ worktree_path }}` on branch `{{ branch_name }}`.
- A dirty working tree is normal here. Read `git status --short` at the start of
  your turn and classify what you find. Keep the changes that belong to this
  issue. Do not reset, discard, or stash work you did not create.
- Stage only the files you edited. Do not use `git add -A`, `git add .`, or
  `git add --all`; they stage every dirty and untracked path in the tree,
  including state that is not yours.
- Commit with `git commit -S -s -m "<conventional subject>"`. The repository
  requires a signed commit and exactly one `Signed-off-by:` trailer that matches
  your author identity.
- Do not change `user.email` or `user.name`. A fabricated identity makes the
  commit unverifiable and fails the signed-commit gate.
- Push the branch when your work is ready. Do not run `gh pr merge`, and do not
  enable, disable, or adopt native auto-merge. The queue's `pr_review` stage
  applies the loop-owned `state:implementation-go` label after its review, and
  the host enables auto-merge after that approval.

